#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560176 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(x + y)\\,i+(- 4 x + 3 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,y=t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(x + y)\\,i+(- 4 x + 3 y)\\,j \\\\  & =((t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}) + (t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}))\\,i+(- 4 (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}) + 3 (t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}))\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\  & =(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =((t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(3 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j)\\cdot ((- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j) \\\\  & =(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+(3 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\  & =(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+(- 4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})+(3 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\  & =- t^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t^{2} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 t^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 t \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + t \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\  & =t^{2} \\left(- \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) + t \\left(3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F(r)\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}(t^{2} \\left(- \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) + t \\left(3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right))\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560177 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(y^{2})\\,i+(- 2 x^{2})\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t^{4})\\,i+(t^{3})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,3]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t^{4},\\,y=t^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(y^{2})\\,i+(- 2 x^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =((t^{3})^{2})\\,i+(- 2 (t^{4})^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(t^{6})\\,i+(- 2 t^{8})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{4})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{3})j \\\\  & =(4 t^{3})\\,i+(3 t^{2})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =((t^{6})\\,i+(- 2 t^{8})\\,j)\\cdot ((4 t^{3})\\,i+(3 t^{2})\\,j) \\\\  & =(t^{6})\\cdot (4 t^{3})+(- 2 t^{8})\\cdot (3 t^{2}) \\\\  & =- 6 t^{10} + 4 t^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F(r)\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{3}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}(- 6 t^{10} + 4 t^{9})\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560183 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(y)\\,i+(- x)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,y=\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(y)\\,i+(- x)\\,j \\\\  & =((\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}))\\,i+(- (\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}))\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\  & =(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =((\\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(- \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j)\\cdot ((- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j) \\\\  & =(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})+(- \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F(r)\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}(-1)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560194 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- y^{2})\\,i+(4 x^{2})\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t^{2})\\,i+(t^{3})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t^{2},\\,y=t^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(- y^{2})\\,i+(4 x^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(- (t^{3})^{2})\\,i+(4 (t^{2})^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(- t^{6})\\,i+(4 t^{4})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{2})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{3})j \\\\  & =(2 t)\\,i+(3 t^{2})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =((- t^{6})\\,i+(4 t^{4})\\,j)\\cdot ((2 t)\\,i+(3 t^{2})\\,j) \\\\  & =(- t^{6})\\cdot (2 t)+(4 t^{4})\\cdot (3 t^{2}) \\\\  & =- 2 t^{7} + 12 t^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F(r)\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}(- 2 t^{7} + 12 t^{6})\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560198 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(x + 2 y)\\,i+(x + 3 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,y=t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(x + 2 y)\\,i+(x + 3 y)\\,j \\\\  & =((t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}) + 2 (t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}))\\,i+((t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}) + 3 (t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}))\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\  & =(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =((2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(3 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j)\\cdot ((- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j) \\\\  & =(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+(3 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\  & =(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})+(3 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\  & =- 2 t^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t^{2} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + t^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 t \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + t \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\  & =t^{2} \\left(- 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) + t \\left(3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F(r)\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}}(t^{2} \\left(- 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) + t \\left(3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right))\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560215 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(x y^{2})\\,i+(2 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(e^{t})\\,i+(\\sqrt{t})\\,j$ for $t\\in [1,3]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=e^{t},\\,y=\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(x y^{2})\\,i+(2 y)\\,j \\\\  & =((e^{t}) (\\sqrt{t})^{2})\\,i+(2 (\\sqrt{t}))\\,j \\\\  & =(t e^{t})\\,i+(2 \\sqrt{t})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{t})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})j \\\\  & =(e^{t})\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t}}\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =((t e^{t})\\,i+(2 \\sqrt{t})\\,j)\\cdot ((e^{t})\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t}}\\right)\\,j) \\\\  & =(t e^{t})\\cdot (e^{t})+(2 \\sqrt{t})\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t}}\\right) \\\\  & =t e^{2 t} + 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F(r)\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{1}^{3}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}(t e^{2 t} + 1)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560219 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 2 x + 4 y)\\,i+(2 x + 3 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi}{3}]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,y=t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(- 2 x + 4 y)\\,i+(2 x + 3 y)\\,j \\\\  & =(- 2 (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}) + 4 (t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}))\\,i+(2 (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}) + 3 (t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}))\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\  & =(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =((4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(3 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j)\\cdot ((- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j) \\\\  & =(4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+(3 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\  & =(- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+(4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+(2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})+(3 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\  & =- 4 t^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 t^{2} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 t \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 6 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 t \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\  & =t^{2} \\left(- 4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) + t \\left(3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F(r)\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}(t^{2} \\left(- 4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) + t \\left(3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right))\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560226 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= (y + 1)\\ln (x + 4),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y + 1)\\ln (x + 4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y + 1}{x + 4} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y + 1)\\ln (x + 4)) \\\\  & =\\ln(x + 4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y + 1}{x + 4} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 4) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y + 1}{x + 4} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 4) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\mathbf i + 0\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y + 1}{x + 4}=0 \\\\ \\ln(x + 4)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y + 1=0,$ which means $y=-1.$ From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x + 4)=0,$ which means that $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf 0$ at the point $(-3,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560229 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= (y - 3)\\ln (x + 2),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y - 3)\\ln (x + 2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y - 3}{x + 2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y - 3)\\ln (x + 2)) \\\\  & =\\ln(x + 2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y - 3}{x + 2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 2) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y - 3}{x + 2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 2) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\mathbf i + 0\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y - 3}{x + 2}=0 \\\\ \\ln(x + 2)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y - 3=0,$ which means $y=3.$ From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x + 2)=0,$ which means that $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf 0$ at the point $(-1,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560248 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= (y + 3)\\ln (x - 2),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y + 3)\\ln (x - 2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y + 3}{x - 2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y + 3)\\ln (x - 2)) \\\\  & =\\ln(x - 2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y + 3}{x - 2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x - 2) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y + 3}{x - 2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x - 2) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\mathbf i + 0\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y + 3}{x - 2}=0 \\\\ \\ln(x - 2)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y + 3=0,$ which means $y=-3.$ From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x - 2)=0,$ which means that $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf 0$ at the point $(3,-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560249 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= (y - 1)\\ln (x + 2),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y - 1)\\ln (x + 2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y - 1}{x + 2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y - 1)\\ln (x + 2)) \\\\  & =\\ln(x + 2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y - 1}{x + 2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 2) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y - 1}{x + 2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 2) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\mathbf i + 0\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y - 1}{x + 2}=0 \\\\ \\ln(x + 2)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y - 1=0,$ which means $y=1.$ From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x + 2)=0,$ which means that $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf 0$ at the point $(-1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560254 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= (y - 3)\\ln (x + 4),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y - 3)\\ln (x + 4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y - 3}{x + 4} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y - 3)\\ln (x + 4)) \\\\  & =\\ln(x + 4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y - 3}{x + 4} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 4) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y - 3}{x + 4} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 4) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\mathbf i + 0\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y - 3}{x + 4}=0 \\\\ \\ln(x + 4)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y - 3=0,$ which means $y=3.$ From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x + 4)=0,$ which means that $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf 0$ at the point $(-3,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560308 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=x^{2} y^{2} z^{2}$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2 u^{2},\\,v,\\,v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ Assume $u\\geq0.$ What is the missing expression? Choose an integrand equal to the required expression as a function on $D$.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=4 \\sqrt{2} u.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=4 \\sqrt{2} u.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x^{2} y^{2} z^{2})\\,{|}_{x=2 u^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =(2 u^{2}\\cdot 2 u^{2})\\cdot v^{2}\\cdot v^{2} \\\\  & =4 u^{4} v^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4 \\sqrt{2} u 4 u^{4} v^{4}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560315 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=- 4 x^{2} + y z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,u + v,\\,v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression? Choose an integrand equal to the required expression as a function on $D$.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8 v^{2} + 1}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8 v^{2} + 1}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(- 4 x^{2} + y z)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=u + v\\,z=v^{2}} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (u\\cdot u)+(u+v)\\cdot v^{2} \\\\  & =- 4 u^{2} + 1 \\left(u + v\\right) v^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\left(- 4 u^{2} + 1 \\left(u + v\\right) v^{2}\\right) \\sqrt{8 v^{2} + 1}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560320 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=3 x - y^{2} z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2 u v,\\,- 3 u,\\,2 u - 2 v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression? Choose an integrand equal to the required expression as a function on $D$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- 3 u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 v,\\,-3,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- 3 u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 u,\\,0,\\,- 4 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 v & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 u & 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2 v & 2 \\\\ 2 u & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 v & -3 \\\\ 2 u & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12 v,\\,4 u + 8 v^{2},\\,6 u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨12 v,\\,4 u + 8 v^{2},\\,6 u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(12 v)^{2}+(4 u + 8 v^{2})^{2}+(6 u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{52 u^{2} + 64 u v^{2} + 64 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(3 x - y^{2} z)\\,{|}_{x=2 u v\\,y=- 3 u\\,z=2 u - 2 v^{2}} \\\\  & =6 u v-1\\cdot (- 3 u)^{2}\\cdot (2 u - 2 v^{2}) \\\\  & =- 18 u^{3} + 18 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\left(- 18 u^{3} + 18 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\sqrt{52 u^{2} + 64 u v^{2} + 64 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560324 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=x - 3 y^{2} z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u v,\\,- 3 u,\\,3 u - 2 v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression? Choose an integrand equal to the required expression as a function on $D$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- 3 u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v,\\,-3,\\,3⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- 3 u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,0,\\,- 4 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ v & -3 & 3 \\\\ u & 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}v & 3 \\\\ u & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}v & -3 \\\\ u & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12 v,\\,3 u + 4 v^{2},\\,3 u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨12 v,\\,3 u + 4 v^{2},\\,3 u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(12 v)^{2}+(3 u + 4 v^{2})^{2}+(3 u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18 u^{2} + 24 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x - 3 y^{2} z)\\,{|}_{x=u v\\,y=- 3 u\\,z=3 u - 2 v^{2}} \\\\  & =u v-3\\cdot (- 3 u)^{2}\\cdot (3 u - 2 v^{2}) \\\\  & =- 81 u^{3} + 54 u^{2} v^{2} + u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\left(- 81 u^{3} + 54 u^{2} v^{2} + u v\\right) \\sqrt{18 u^{2} + 24 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560328 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=x - y^{2} z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2 u v,\\,- u,\\,3 u - v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression? Choose an integrand equal to the required expression as a function on $D$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3 u - v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 v,\\,-1,\\,3⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3 u - v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 u,\\,0,\\,- 2 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 v & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 u & 0 & - 2 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & - 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2 v & 3 \\\\ 2 u & - 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 v & -1 \\\\ 2 u & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 v,\\,6 u + 4 v^{2},\\,2 u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2 v,\\,6 u + 4 v^{2},\\,2 u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2 v)^{2}+(6 u + 4 v^{2})^{2}+(2 u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40 u^{2} + 48 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 4 v^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x - y^{2} z)\\,{|}_{x=2 u v\\,y=- u\\,z=3 u - v^{2}} \\\\  & =2 u v-1\\cdot (- u)^{2}\\cdot (3 u - v^{2}) \\\\  & =- 3 u^{3} + u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\left(- 3 u^{3} + u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\sqrt{40 u^{2} + 48 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 4 v^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560333 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=2 x - y^{2} z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨3 u v,\\,- u,\\,2 u - 2 v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression? Choose an integrand equal to the required expression as a function on $D$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3 u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3 v,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3 u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3 u,\\,0,\\,- 4 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 v & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 u & 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}3 v & 2 \\\\ 3 u & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}3 v & -1 \\\\ 3 u & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4 v,\\,6 u + 12 v^{2},\\,3 u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨4 v,\\,6 u + 12 v^{2},\\,3 u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4 v)^{2}+(6 u + 12 v^{2})^{2}+(3 u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45 u^{2} + 144 u v^{2} + 144 v^{4} + 16 v^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(2 x - y^{2} z)\\,{|}_{x=3 u v\\,y=- u\\,z=2 u - 2 v^{2}} \\\\  & =6 u v-1\\cdot (- u)^{2}\\cdot (2 u - 2 v^{2}) \\\\  & =- 2 u^{3} + 2 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\left(- 2 u^{3} + 2 u^{2} v^{2} + 6 u v\\right) \\sqrt{45 u^{2} + 144 u v^{2} + 144 v^{4} + 16 v^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560336 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=2 x - 2 y^{2} z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u v,\\,- 3 u,\\,2 u - 2 v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression? Choose an integrand equal to the required expression as a function on $D$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- 3 u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v,\\,-3,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- 3 u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 u - 2 v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,0,\\,- 4 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ v & -3 & 2 \\\\ u & 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}v & 2 \\\\ u & - 4 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}v & -3 \\\\ u & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12 v,\\,2 u + 4 v^{2},\\,3 u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨12 v,\\,2 u + 4 v^{2},\\,3 u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(12 v)^{2}+(2 u + 4 v^{2})^{2}+(3 u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13 u^{2} + 16 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(2 x - 2 y^{2} z)\\,{|}_{x=u v\\,y=- 3 u\\,z=2 u - 2 v^{2}} \\\\  & =2 u v-2\\cdot (- 3 u)^{2}\\cdot (2 u - 2 v^{2}) \\\\  & =- 36 u^{3} + 36 u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\left(- 36 u^{3} + 36 u^{2} v^{2} + 2 u v\\right) \\sqrt{13 u^{2} + 16 u v^{2} + 16 v^{4} + 144 v^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560984 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}-y,\\hspace{0.222em}x{e}^{y}⟩$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y},\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩$\n\nIII. $$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨-\\frac{y}{5}+{x}^{7},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{6x}{5}⟩\n$$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\hspace{0.222em}Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x+Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I give the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{y},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{e}^{y}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{5}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{6}{5},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{7}{5}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561018 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\nI. $P(x,y)=-\\frac{y}{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\frac{x}{3}$\n\nII. $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n\nIII. $P(x,y)={y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{3}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{3},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{2}{3}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III does *not* give the required area. Notice that \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x-2y\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561027 :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5eb703c2812d82848bb5a1639c2840c4abf9318b988087e2216d9881137da72.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)={t}^{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [-1,1],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t)=\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}-t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ \n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and   \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\hspace{1em} & {\\mathbf{r}}_{1}^{'}(t)=4{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\hspace{1em} & {\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t)=-\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\\,dx+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{3}\\cdot 4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{6}\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & =4[\\frac{{t}^{7}}{7}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561062 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\nI. $P(x,y)={e}^{y}$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}$\n\nII. $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=y$\n\nIII. $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=y$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x}-{e}^{y}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II does *not* give the required area.  Notice that \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{2}}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{2}}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561063 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\nI. $P(x,y)=y$ and $Q(x,y)=x$\n\nII. $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0$\n\nIII. $P(x,y)=y+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{3}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561104 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=2 x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,2),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_C\\left(2 x\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=2 x^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 t^{2}\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(4 t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(2 x\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(2 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(2 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(2 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(2 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(2 \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(2 \\left(2 t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(4 t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}2 t+16 t^{3}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}16 t^{3} + 2 t\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[t^{2}+4 t^{4}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\  &=[\\left(1\\right)^{2}+4 \\left(1\\right)^{4}]-[\\left(0\\right)^{2}+4 \\left(0\\right)^{4}] \\\\  &=[1+4]-[0+0] \\\\  &=5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561108 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=3 x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,3),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_C\\left(y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 x\\right)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=3 x^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 t^{2}\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(6 t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 x\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(3 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(3 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(3 t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(3 \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(6 t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}3 t^{2}+18 t^{2}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}21 t^{2}\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[7 t^{3}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\  &=[7 \\left(1\\right)^{3}]-[7 \\left(0\\right)^{3}] \\\\  &=[7]-[0] \\\\  &=7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561110 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=3 \\pi x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(2,12 \\pi),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_C\\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{y}{x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\,dy=\n$$ The composed integrand at the initial endpoint is interpreted by its one-sided continuous extension along $C.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=3 \\pi x^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 \\pi t^{2}\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(6 \\pi t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{y}{x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{y}{x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{y}{x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{\\left(3 \\pi t^{2}\\right)}{\\left(t\\right)^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(\\left(t\\right)^{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(6 \\pi t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}-1+6 \\pi t^{3}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}6 \\pi t^{3} - 1\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[- t+\\frac{3 \\pi t^{4}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\  &=[- \\left(2\\right)+\\frac{3 \\pi \\left(2\\right)^{4}}{2}]-[- \\left(0\\right)+\\frac{3 \\pi \\left(0\\right)^{4}}{2}] \\\\  &=[-2+24 \\pi]-[0+0] \\\\  &=-2 + 24 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561117 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,1),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_C\\left(3 y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 x\\right)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=x^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(3 y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 x\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(3 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(2 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(3 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(2 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(3 \\left(t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(2 \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}3 t^{2}+4 t^{2}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}7 t^{2}\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[\\frac{7 t^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\  &=[\\frac{7 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3}]-[\\frac{7 \\left(0\\right)^{3}}{3}] \\\\  &=[\\frac{7}{3}]-[0] \\\\  &=\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561123 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=x^{2} - 3$ traversed from the point $A(0,-3)$ to point $B(2,1),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_C\\left(x^{2} y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 x^{2} \\left(y + 3\\right)\\right)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=x^{2} - 3.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{2} - 3\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(x^{2} y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 x^{2} \\left(y + 3\\right)\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(x^{2} y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(3 x^{2} \\left(y + 3\\right)\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(x^{2} y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(3 x^{2} \\left(y + 3\\right)\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(t\\right)^{2} \\left(t^{2} - 3\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(3 \\left(t\\right)^{2} \\left(\\left(t^{2} - 3\\right) + 3\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}t^{4} - 3 t^{2}+6 t^{5}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}6 t^{5} + t^{4} - 3 t^{2}\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[\\frac{t^{5}}{5}- t^{3}+t^{6}\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\  &=[\\frac{\\left(2\\right)^{5}}{5}- \\left(2\\right)^{3}+\\left(2\\right)^{6}]-[\\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{5}}{5}- \\left(0\\right)^{3}+\\left(0\\right)^{6}] \\\\  &=[\\frac{32}{5}-8+64]-[0+0+0] \\\\  &=\\frac{312}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561136 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=3 x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,3),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_C\\left(x\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 y\\right)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=3 x^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 t^{2}\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(6 t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(x\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 y\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(3 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(3 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(3 \\left(3 t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(6 t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}t+54 t^{3}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}54 t^{3} + t\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[\\frac{t^{2}}{2}+\\frac{27 t^{4}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\  &=[\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{27 \\left(1\\right)^{4}}{2}]-[\\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{27 \\left(0\\right)^{4}}{2}] \\\\  &=[\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{27}{2}]-[0+0] \\\\  &=14\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561139 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,1),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_C\\left(3 x\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=x^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(3 x\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(3 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(2 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(3 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(2 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(3 \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(2 \\left(t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}3 t+4 t^{3}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}4 t^{3} + 3 t\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}+t^{4}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\  &=[\\frac{3 \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\left(1\\right)^{4}]-[\\frac{3 \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\left(0\\right)^{4}] \\\\  &=[\\frac{3}{2}+1]-[0+0] \\\\  &=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561153 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=3 \\pi x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(1,3 \\pi)$ to point $B(3,27 \\pi),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_C\\left(\\sin{\\left(\\frac{y}{2 x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x\\right)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=3 \\pi x^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 \\pi t^{2}\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,3]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(6 \\pi t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(\\sin{\\left(\\frac{y}{2 x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\left(\\sin{\\left(\\frac{y}{2 x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\sin{\\left(\\frac{y}{2 x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\left(\\sin{\\left(\\frac{\\left(3 \\pi t^{2}\\right)}{2 \\left(t\\right)^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(\\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(6 \\pi t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{1}^{3}-1+6 \\pi t^{2}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{1}^{3}6 \\pi t^{2} - 1\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[- t+2 \\pi t^{3}\\right]_{1}^{3} \\\\  &=[- \\left(3\\right)+2 \\pi \\left(3\\right)^{3}]-[- \\left(1\\right)+2 \\pi \\left(1\\right)^{3}] \\\\  &=[-3+54 \\pi]-[-1+2 \\pi] \\\\  &=-2 + 52 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561161 :question/problem "![](images/05/0567f92e27f3a9699065515bd8230ac21154de2790d08433de65d4d783187b78.png)\n\nThe axes have half-unit ticks, with even integers labeled on the grid margins. The ellipse is centered at the origin. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the marked point $A$?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=9$ and $b=\\frac{13}{2}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561163 :question/problem "![](images/2d/2da106244edda5674f146eef5f6de94d4ce51490d6a0775289de2c5c3a18c832.png)\n\nThe axes have half-unit ticks, with even integers labeled on the grid margins. The ellipse is centered at the origin. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the marked point $A$?\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=9$ and $b=\\frac{7}{2}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561168 :question/problem "![](images/dc/dc558909c1618e193156b698a5bf54580d9ba56437947c15570770976e07c4d0.png)\n\nThe axes have half-unit ticks, with even integers labeled on the grid margins. The ellipse is centered at the origin. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the marked point $A$?\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{17}{2}$ and $b=\\frac{13}{2}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{17}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561177 :question/problem "![](images/d6/d6ce8433138f0011bc35ff94a1d514b031053a535849ffb8a3f2ecf0e2657937.png)\n\nThe axes have half-unit ticks, with even integers labeled on the grid margins. The ellipse is centered at the origin. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the marked point $A$?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=\\frac{15}{2}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561184 :question/problem "![](images/c2/c252b1a914c9770f46e5defed9c9a960a2a04d2a2b6613125b7c25b471041b15.png)\n\nThe axes have half-unit ticks, with even integers labeled on the grid margins. The ellipse is centered at the origin. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the marked point $A$?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{17}{2}$ and $b=\\frac{7}{2}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{17}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561273 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}} \\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} 2 r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}} \\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} 2 r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta & = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}} 2 \\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}} 2 \\left[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}} 2 \\left(\\frac{\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\right)^{2}-\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}} 4 \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the double-angle formula\n\n$$\n\\cos(2x)=2\\cos^2 x-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2\\theta$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\left(2\\theta\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(1+\\cos\\left(4\\theta\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can finish our integral by integrating with respect to $\\theta,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}} 4 \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta & = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}} 2\\left(1+\\cos\\left(4\\theta\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = 2\\left[\\theta + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 \\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}} \\\\\n& = 2\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{12}+\\frac{1}{4}\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\right)-\\left(0+\\frac{1}{4}\\sin\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = 2\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{12}+\\frac{1}{4} \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(0+\\frac{1}{4} \\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = 2\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{12}\\right)+\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561290 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}} \\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}} \\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta & = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}} \\left[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}} \\left(\\frac{\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\right)^{2}-\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}} 2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the double-angle formula\n\n$$\n\\cos(2x)=2\\cos^2 x-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2\\theta$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\left(2\\theta\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(1+\\cos\\left(4\\theta\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can finish our integral by integrating with respect to $\\theta,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}} 2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta & = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}} 1\\left(1+\\cos\\left(4\\theta\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = 1\\left[\\theta + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 \\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}} \\\\\n& = 1\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{12}+\\frac{1}{4}\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\right)-\\left(0+\\frac{1}{4}\\sin\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = 1\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{12}+\\frac{1}{4} \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(0+\\frac{1}{4} \\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = 1\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{12}\\right)+\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} + \\frac{\\pi}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561292 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} \\int_{2}^{4} r^{3} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} \\int_{2}^{4} r^{3} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta & = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\int_{2}^{4} r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\left[\\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\right]_{2}^{4}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\left(\\frac{\\left(4\\right)^{4}-\\left(2\\right)^{4}}{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} 60 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} 60 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta & = \\left[60 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} \\\\\n& = \\left(\\left(60 \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right) \\right)}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(60 \\sin{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = \\left(\\left(30 \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = 30 \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
